	Then $\Pi_{\mathrm{sMH}}$ is $(\mu,s)$-reversible if and only if the balancing condition \eqref{eq:andrieu2020-balancing-condition} holds.
\end{corollary}

expanded_sketch:
The post-theorem text does not give a step-by-step proof of the main theorem, but it outlines how the paper obtains it “synthetically using Markov and CD categories” and what the main ingredients are:
\begin{itemize}
\item Use \emph{Markov categories} “to formulate generic aspects of MCMC algorithms, such as invariance, reversibility and state-space augmentation.”
\item Use \emph{CD categories} “to reason about the first summand in
\begin{equation} \label{eq:mh-kernel}
	\Pimh(\xi, \dif \xi') \coloneqq \alpha(\xi) \, \delta_{\phi(\xi)}(\dif \xi') + (1 - \alpha(\xi)) \, \delta_{\xi}(\dif \xi'),
\end{equation}
: we prove reversibility of this term, and recover part of the condition
\begin{equation} \label{eq:balancing-ae}
		\alpha(\xi) = \begin{cases}
			\alpha(\phi(\xi)) \, r(\xi) & \text{if $\xi \in S$,} \\
			0 & \text{otherwise}.
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
.”
\item To handle the \emph{entire} MH kernel $\Pimh$, add structure by working with CD categories “\emph{enriched} over commutative monoids,” needed to “\`add' morphisms” and to “reason about decompositions of morphisms into disjoint \`parts'." In the context of establishing the main theorem, this enrichment “allows us to reason about decompositions of $\mu$ into parts supported on $S$ and $S^c$, and to obtain the condition
\begin{equation} \label{eq:balancing-ae}
		\alpha(\xi) = \begin{cases}
			\alpha(\phi(\xi)) \, r(\xi) & \text{if $\xi \in S$,} \\
			0 & \text{otherwise}.
		\end{cases}
	\end{equation}
via purely algebraic arguments.”
\end{itemize}
It also notes that this approach “supplies a converse to the main theorem” and “permits a straightforward extension” to the skew-reversible setting.,

