The introduction explains that the proof of the main theorem is "based on a careful treatment of martingales and not on the approach of group actions". The key stated ingredient is: "The main technical lemma states that asymptotically, the values of a bounded harmonic function observed by a random walk do not change if one adds an additional, constant step \textit{at the end} of the walk." We first record this proposition.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:theBasicLemma}
    Let $G$, $\mu$ and $\nu_\mu$ be as above. Let $f\colon G\lora\C$ be a measurable function such that for $\nu_\mu$-almost every trajectory $\ler{w_n}_n\in G^\N$, the values $f\ler{w_n}$ converge. Let $m$ be a fixed positive integer. Then for $\nu_\mu\times\mu^{*m}$-almost every pair $\ler{(w_n)_n,\gamma}\in G^\N\times G$, we have
    \begin{equation}\label{converges}
        |f\ler{w_n}-f\ler{w_n\gamma}|\to0 \text{ as } n\to\infty.
    \end{equation}
\end{proposition}

It is also noted that this martingale-based method "allows us to consider non-generating measures $\theta$" and yields the result "for the generality of asymptotic commutativity" and even extends (with adaptations) to "general Markov chains."

