expanded_sketch:
Using the Markov partition/isomorphism for Anosov diffeomorphisms, the proof reduces to the SFT version: “Therefore in order to prove Theorem $1.1$ it is enough to prove” the analogous theorem for a mixing subshift of finite type.

“Following \cite{16}, we reduce the proof of Bernoullicity to finding a generating VWB (very weakly Bernoulli) partition.” A generating partition “$\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{Q}$” is specified (and the argument “works for any smooth (satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{smpart}
    \nu(V_\delta (\partial\mathcal{Q}))\xrightarrow{\delta\to 0}0,
\end{equation}
) partition $\mathcal{Q}$ of the fiber”), so “the main part is the proof of the VWB property.”

VWB is approached by “find[ing] a matching between most atoms of the past partition $\bigvee_{i=0}^\infty T^i(\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{Q})$.” Using “property
\begin{equation}\label{smpart}
    \nu(V_\delta (\partial\mathcal{Q}))\xrightarrow{\delta\to 0}0,
\end{equation}
and the fact that the fiber entropy is 0,” Lemma 2.11 reduces this to “find[ing] a measurable isomorphism between most of the unstable sets $\Phi:\Sigma^+(x)\to\Sigma^+(\bar x)$ in the base,” so that “the fiber coordinates of $T^i(x,z)$ and $T^i(\Phi(x),\bar z)$ stay close to each other for most of the times $i$ in some future interval $[1,N]$.”

Unlike standard VWB matchings that bring points close in time $o(N)$ and then “copy[] the remaining $N-o(N)$ symbols,” here the matching must have “$\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{Q}$ names … agreeing on a family of disjoint short intervals of $i$, each of length $o(N)$,” due to fiber constraints: even with “good equidistribution in the fiber” the time to make two typical fiber points “$\tfrac{1}{N}$-close together is roughly of size $N$,” while Denjoy–Koksma implies points $\tfrac{1}{N}$ apart horizontally “can only travel together … for a distance bounded by $N^{\frac{2}{3}+\epsilon}$” (for $\gamma$ near $\tfrac12$).

The “idea is therefore to construct the matching one block at a time” and “calculate the maximal measure of the domain on which it is possible to shift the Birkhoff sums of the cocycle $S_n\varphi$ by some time $t$.” The “optimal measure, equal to $\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{n}}$, involves studying various limit theorems for conditional measures on the unstable sets $\Sigma^+(x)$” (Section 3). Then one “adjust[s] the two trajectories with the use of the next block” with $t$ chosen as the separation at the end of the previous block; “Section 4 gives upper bounds on these distances.”

Next, the “final matching” is built “using the following lemma on blocks of varying length,” fixing lengths $H_i(N)$ and choosing $n=H_i(N)$ with $t$ so the “loss of measure, which is roughly $\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{H_i(N)}}$, does not exceed the number of blocks.”
\begin{lemma}\label{32final}
    $\forall\epsilon\ \forall\delta\ \exists n_4$ such that for $n\ge n_4$, any $N>0,\ \sqrt{n}/\log^2 n\ge |t|$ and $A\subset\Sigma^+(x),\ \bar A\subset\Sigma^+(\bar x)$ which are $(k,N)*$ with $k+n\le L(N)$ there are finite partitions $\mathcal{P},\ \mathcal{\bar P}$  of $A,\ \bar A$ into $((k+n),N)*$ sets and a measurable isomorphism $\phi_{A,\bar A}^{t,n}:A\rightarrow\bar A$ such that \\
    1. $\phi_{A,\bar A}^{t,n}(\mathcal{P})=\mathcal{\bar P}$\\
    2. there is a set $$D:=\bigcup_{\text{good}\ P_i\in \mathcal{P}}P_i\text{\quad with\quad}\mu_x^+(D)\ge \left(1-\log^{100} n\frac{t+\log n}{\sqrt{n}}\right)\mu_x^+(A)$$
    such that for any $y\in D$
    \begin{equation}\label{32finalstatement1}
        |S_n\varphi(\sigma^k(\phi_{A,\bar A}^{t,n}(y)))-S_n\varphi(\sigma^k(y))-t|\le\log^{10} n
    \end{equation}
     and for $1-\epsilon^2$ proportion of $ 1\le r\le n$ we have
    \begin{equation}\label{32finalstatement2}
        |S_r\varphi(\sigma^k(\phi_{A,\bar A}^{t,n}(y)))-S_r\varphi(\sigma^k(y))-t|\le \delta\text{\quad and\quad}(\phi_{A,\bar A}^{t,n}(y))_{k+r}=y_{k+r}
    \end{equation}
\end{lemma}
Ensuring the loss is “$o(\tfrac{L(N)}{H_i(N)})$” yields the following lemma, giving controlled shifts of Birkhoff sums “at every step.”
\begin{lemma}\label{52good}
 $\forall \epsilon'>0\ \exists N_0\in\mathbb{N}\ \forall N\ge N_0,\ \forall r,\ \bar r\in\Gamma_1(N),$ we have $\mu_x^+(D_1)\ge1-\epsilon'$
\end{lemma}
The following lemma then bounds “the distances … at the end of each block.”
\begin{lemma}\label{52mapworks}
 $\exists N_0\in\mathbb{N}\ \forall N\ge N_0,\ \forall r,\ \bar r\in\Gamma_1(N),$ such that $r=\Sigma^+(x)\times\{z\},\bar r=\Sigma^+(\bar x)\times\{\bar z\}$ $\forall y\in D_{1}(N)\cap D_0(N)$ such that $(y,z)\in B(N)$ and for all $I_{n+1}=[l_{i}^h(N)+1,l_{i}^{h+1}(N)]$ from $S(y,z,N)$, for some $i,h,n$ and $\bar z_n, z_n$ defined above (appearing in the definition of $\phi_N^{r,\bar r}(y)$), we have $|d_N(\bar z_n,z_n)|\le N^{(\frac{3}{2}-\frac{\eta}{100})e_{i}-1}$
\end{lemma}
Finally, the following lemma shows that for most words most of $[1,L(N)]$ is covered by the shortest blocks where Birkhoff sums are controlled, “which suffices for $o(1)$ separation in the direction of the flow,” completing the VWB verification and hence Bernoullicity.
\begin{lemma}\label{52precel}
    $\forall \epsilon',\delta>0\ \exists N_0\in\mathbb{N}\ \forall N\ge N_0,\ \forall r,\ \bar r\in\Gamma_1(N)$ such that $r=\Sigma^+(x)\times\{z\},\bar r=\Sigma^+(\bar x)\times\{\bar z\}$ $\forall y\in D_{1}(N)\cap D_0(N)$ such that $(y,z)\in B(N)$, we have $$|d_N(K_{S_j\varphi(\phi_N^{r,\bar r}(y))}(\bar z),K_{S_j\varphi(y)}(z))|\le \delta\quad\text{and}\quad y_j=(\phi_N^{r,\bar r}(y))_j \quad  \text{for $(1-\epsilon')$ proportion of}\  j\in[1, L(N)]$$
\end{lemma}

