is given, and it is said to be based on “a novel minimax argument that incorporates the estimates of explicit lower bounds of the generalized Morse index directly into seeking for critical points of the energy functional.” The outline is divided into five parts:

$\langle i\rangle$ \emph{A New Minimax Framework.} The authors “substantiate the existence of critical values for functionals with a mountain pass structure” via a method that “differs fundamentally from the standard one for the proof of the mountain pass theorem,” with the “primary objective” to “distinguish these critical points by estimating their generalized Morse indices.” They explain why “standard minimax theories” (group action / linking) “are not suitable” here since the problems “lack symmetry” and do not “readily admit the verification of nontrivial topology.”

$\langle ii\rangle$ \emph{Smoothing and Finite-Dimensional Approximation.} To get “desired lower bounds for the generalized Morse index” for $J$ on $X$, they “mollify the nonlinearity $f$” to get “smoothed functionals $J_m$,” and for fixed $m$ study $J_m$ on finite-dimensional subspaces $X_n$, denoted $J_{m,n}$.

$\langle iii\rangle$ \emph{The Invariant Minimax Family and Smooth Flow.} A mapping family for $J_m$ is defined on an annulus $D_{r_k,R_k}\subset X_k$ (with $k<n$), and is “invariant under the descending flow of $X_n$.” A key point is establishing “the $C^2$ smoothness of the flow $\tau(t,u)$” on $(\mathbb{R}^+\cup\{0\})\times X_n$ in the initial data. We first prove the following lemma. \begin{lemma}
\label{lem2.1} Assume $\left( g_1\right) $ holds, i.e., $g:X\rightarrow X$
is a $C^2$ mapping. Then the Cauchy problem
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{array}{c}
\frac{d\tau \left( t,u\right) }{dt}=g\left( \tau \left( t,u\right) \right) ,\text{ }\left( t,u\right) \in \Bbb{R}^{+}\times X, \\
\tau \left( 0,u\right) =u\in X,
\end{array}
\right.  \label{eq5}
\end{equation}
has a unique solution $\tau \left( t,u\right) \in C^2\left( \Bbb{R}
^{+}\times X,X\right) $. Furthermore, the derivative with respect to the
initial value, which we denote by $\tau _u^{\prime }\left( t,u\right) $, as
a bounded linear operator from $X$ to $X$, is injective for all $t$, i.e., $ 
\ker \tau _u^{\prime }\left( t,u\right) =\left\{ 0\right\} $. Finally, for
each initial point $u\in X$, if $g\left( \tau \left( t,u\right) \right) =0$
and $g\left( u\right) \neq 0$, then $t=\infty $.
\end{lemma}
In this lemma, “the Gronwall-Bellman inequality first ensures the uniqueness of the flow,” and together with “the $C^2$ smoothness of the governing vector field” yields “the injectivity of its derivative and the flow's $C^2$ smoothness.” For fixed $t>0$, the flow gives a diffeomorphism from $D_{r_k,R_k}$ onto a $C^2$ submanifold $A_t\subset X_n$.

$\langle iv\rangle$ \emph{Critical Points with Index Bounds.} They show the associated minimax value $C_k^{(m,n)}$ “is a critical value of $J_m$ on $X_n$.” By studying “a sequence of maximum points $v_{t_j}$ on $A_{t_j}$ for $t_j\to\infty$,” they obtain a Palais–Smale sequence for $J_m$ on $X_n$. Using the preceding lemma's “triviality of the kernel of $\tau_u'(t,u):X_k\to T_{\tau(t,u)}A_t$,” they get that “the sum of the dimensions of the negative and zero subspaces of the Hessian $J_m''(v_{t_j})$ is at least $k$,” and conclude there is a critical point $u_{m,n,k}$ of $J_m$ on $X_n$ with “$M(J_{m,n},u_{m,n,k})\ge k$.”

$\langle v\rangle$ \emph{Limit Passage.} A “final two-step limit process,” first $m\to\infty$ then $n\to\infty$, using the (PS)$^{**}$ and (PS)$^*$ conditions, “yields a sequence of critical points $\{u_k\}_{k=1}^\infty$ for the functional $J$ satisfying $M(J,u_k)\ge k$, and thus completes the proof of the main theorems.”

